EXTRA_INTERP = {
 "Fig09": "Resistomes were broad in all four cities: pooled across samples, MLS (17.6% of total RPKM), multi-class (16.9%), aminoglycoside (15.6%), beta-lactam (14.1%) and tetracycline (11.7%) genes accounted for most abundance. Median total abundance was highest in Delhi (666 RPKM; IQR 566–820) and lowest in Mumbai (421; 335–519). With sites as units, the city difference was supported (H = 9.9; permutation p = 0.005), and the mixed model gave fold changes relative to Delhi of 0.62 (95% CI 0.48–0.78) for Chennai, 0.62 (0.50–0.77) for Mumbai and 0.81 (0.59–1.10) for Kolkata. The data cannot attribute the difference to antibiotic use, sewerage or hydraulics, none of which were measured. Limitation: producer-processed CARD/RGI output without a reported CARD release; Kolkata has two sites.",
 "Fig26": "Resistome dissimilarity increased with distance (Mantel r = 0.20; p = 0.0003), mainly within the first few thousand kilometres, after which bin medians levelled off, suggesting regional rather than continuous global structuring. Total abundance showed weak-to-moderate positive spatial autocorrelation (Moran's I = 0.18; permutation p = 0.0024; k = 5), robust to k = 8 (I = 0.14). These patterns are compatible with shared regional drivers such as antibiotic consumption and sanitation, which were not analysed. Limitation: one 2016 sampling round with mostly one sample per city and uneven spacing; results apply to municipal sewage only and do not identify hotspots.",
}
